menu "espix networking"

    choice ESPIX_USB_ROLE
        prompt "What the USB-OTG port is"
        depends on SOC_USB_OTG_SUPPORTED
        default ESPIX_USB_ROLE_HOST
        help
            A board with one general-purpose USB controller cannot be both a
            device and a host: the OTG peripheral is a singleton, and
            SOC_USB_OTG_PERIPH_NUM above says how many this chip has. espix picks
            one role at build time rather than pretending otherwise, because the
            two stacks do not coexist in the link either.

            The P4 is the only espix target with two (SOC_USB_OTG_PERIPH_NUM is
            2 there), and this choice still applies to it: the wiring of one
            board's two sockets is not something software can discover.

    config ESPIX_USB_ROLE_HOST
        bool "host -- espix drives attached devices"
        help
            The OTG port powers and enumerates devices plugged into it: storage
            first (`lsblk`, `blkid`), a keyboard later. This is the default
            because a machine that calls itself a server should have a host
            port.

            Two consequences worth knowing before flashing it. usb0 disappears
            from the image, so a board that was reachable over the cable is not
            any more -- WiFi and SSH are the way in. And on a devkit whose two
            sockets sit close together, a hub in the OTG socket can block the
            UART socket, which leaves SSH as the only console; unplugging the
            hub is the recovery, not a reflash.

    config ESPIX_USB_ROLE_DEVICE
        bool "device -- USB-NCM ethernet (usb0)"
        help
            Presents the board to a computer as a USB Ethernet adapter. See
            `espix networking` below for the mode and address options; they are
            unchanged, they simply only apply to this role.

    endchoice

    config ESPIX_USB_NCM_ENABLED
        bool "USB-NCM (Ethernet over the USB-OTG port)"
        default y
        depends on ESPIX_USB_ROLE_DEVICE
        help
            Presents the board to a computer as a USB Ethernet adapter, so it
            is reachable over the cable with no WiFi, no access point and no
            router. The interface is usb0, and it goes up and down as the cable
            is plugged and unplugged.

            This does not touch the console. espix's console is the UART, with
            USB-Serial-JTAG as a secondary; neither uses the USB-OTG peripheral
            this claims, and on a devkit with two sockets both work at once.

            Turning it off drops espix's driver and the whole TinyUSB stack from
            the link, which is where the flash and RAM go. The component is
            still downloaded either way -- the component manager resolves
            manifests before Kconfig exists, so it cannot see this option.

    choice ESPIX_USB_NCM_MODE_DEFAULT
        prompt "Default mode, before /etc/usb.conf exists"
        depends on ESPIX_USB_NCM_ENABLED
        default ESPIX_USB_NCM_MODE_SERVER
        help
            What usb0 does on a device that has never been configured.
            /etc/usb.conf overrides this, and `usb mode` writes that file.

        config ESPIX_USB_NCM_MODE_SERVER
            bool "server -- espix hands the computer an address"
            help
                espix takes a fixed address and runs a DHCP server on usb0, so
                plugging the cable in is all it takes to reach the board. The
                offer carries an address and netmask only: no router and no DNS,
                because espix is not a router and a gateway in that offer can
                take over the computer's default route and break its internet.

        config ESPIX_USB_NCM_MODE_CLIENT
            bool "client -- espix asks the computer's network for one"
            help
                espix runs a DHCP client on usb0. For a computer that bridges or
                shares its own network over the USB link, which is how the board
                joins a LAN through the machine it is plugged into.

    endchoice

    config ESPIX_USB_NCM_ADDRESS
        string "Address for server mode"
        depends on ESPIX_USB_NCM_ENABLED
        default "192.168.7.1"
        help
            espix's own address on usb0 in server mode; the computer is offered
            one from the same /24.

            192.168.7.x rather than 192.168.4.x, which is ESP-IDF's WiFi access
            point default -- keeping them apart means a future AP cannot collide
            with the USB link.

endmenu
